          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong Wightman axioms
            are an attempt to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom"} axiomatize
            and thus formalize the notion of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+quantum+field+theory"}> relativistic quantum field theory
            ) in the sense of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            to points or subsets of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"}> operator-valued distributions
            ).
          %p
            They serve as the basis of what is known as
            %strong constructive quantum field theory
            which seeks to provide a mathematically sound framework for quantum theory over the Minkowski space background of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+relativity"} special relativity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Wightman"} Arthur Wightman
            first formulated them in the 1950s but they were not published until 1964 after advances in scattering theory confirmed their applicability.
          %p
            The Wightman axioms served to establish rigorously several basic structural properties of quantum field theories on Minkowski spacetime, such as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Osterwalder%E2%80%93Schrader+theorem"} Osterwalder–Schrader theorem
            relating Lorenzian and Euclidean quantum field theories (“Wick rotation”).
          %p
            They were later further abstracted to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            that characterize
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} algebraic quantum field theory
          %p This page is a draft, see:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia on the
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wightman_axioms"} Wightman axioms
          %h2#wightman_axioms Wightman Axioms
          %p Note: the numbering - and indeed the actual number - of axioms varies depending on the source. In many sources, several of the axioms below are combined.
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom Axiom
            %p
              There is a physical Hilbert space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              in which a unitary representation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(a,\Lambda)
              of the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+spinor+group"} Poincaré spinor group
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{0}
              acts.
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_2 Axiom
            %p
              The spectrum of the energy-momentum operator P is concentrated in the closed upper (forward) light cone
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi V
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^{+}
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_3 Axiom
            %p
              There exists in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℋ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              a unique unit vector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |0\rangle
              (the
              = succeed ")," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(a,1)
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_4 Axiom
            %p
              The components
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{i}
              of the quantum field
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/operator-valued+distributions"} operator-valued distributions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{i}(x)
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+space"} Schwartz space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(M)
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tempered+distributions"}> tempered distributions
              ) with domain of definition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              which is common to all the operators and is dense in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |0\rangle
              is contained in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              is taken into itself under the action of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi (f)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Λ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(a,\Lambda)
          %p
            Note: As in distribution theory it is custom to abuse the notation and write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi (x)
            for a point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of the Minkowski spacetime and talk about the function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(f)
            of a test function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_5 Axiom
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Λ
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ij
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Λ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Λ
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(a,\Lambda)\phi_{i}(x)U(a,\Lambda)^{-1}=\sum_{j}V_{ij}(\Lambda^{-1})\phi_{j}(\Lambda x + a)
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ij
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_{ij}(\Lambda)
              is a complex or real finite-dimensional matrix representation of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SL
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SL(2,C)
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_6 Axiom
            %p
              Any two field components
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{i}(x)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{j}(y)
              either commute or anticommute under a space-like separation of the arguments
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          .num_defn
            %h6#axiom_7 Axiom
            %p
              The set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_{0}
              of finite linear combinations of vectors of the form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi …
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{i_1}(f_{1})\ldots\phi_{i_n}(f_{n})|0\rangle
              is dense in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℋ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#the_wightman_reconstruction_theorem The Wightman Reconstruction Theorem
          %p
            The
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+values"} vacuum expectation values
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"}> n-point functions
            ) of the theory are all (tempered, by the axioms) distributions of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi i
                        %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\langle0| \phi_{i_1}(f_{1})\ldots\phi_{i_n}(f_{n})|0\rangle
          %p
            The term “distribution” is used interchangably with “function” and “value” in the physics literature. The Wightman reconstruction theorem states properties that a set of tempered distributions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝒲
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi n
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\mathcal{W}^n | n \in \N \}
            need to have to be the set of vacuum expectation values of a Wightman theory, and all such Wightman theories are then unitarily equivalent. …to be continued…
          %h3#equivalence_to_euclidean_field_theory Equivalence to Euclidean Field Theory
          %p
            See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Osterwalder%E2%80%93Schrader+theorem"} Osterwalder–Schrader theorem
          %h3#equivalence_to_the_haagkastler_axioms Equivalence to the Haag–Kastler Axioms
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag%E2%80%93Kastler+axioms"} Haag–Kastler axioms
          %ul
            %li
              %p They are equivalent.
            %li
              %p One contains the other, i.e. one set of axioms implies the other.
            %li
              %p At least one is wrong (from the physical viewpoint).
          %p Unfortunatly the situation does not seem to be as clear as this list suggests. The current state of the affair seems to be that
          %ul
            %li “the Haag-Kastler approach is more convenient, because it deals with algebras of bounded operators, while Wightman fields are allowed to generate unbounded operators”
          %p and
          %ul
            %li “a Wightman field theory is equivalent to a Haag-Kastler theory, if some mild additional assumptions are made”.
          %p Reference:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans-J%C3%BCrgen+Borchers"}> Hans-Jürgen Borchers
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jakob+Yngvason"} Jakob Yngvason
              = succeed "," do
                %em From quantum fields to local von Neumann algebras
              %strong 4
              spec01 (1992) 15-47 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/S0129055X92000145"}> doi:10.1142/S0129055X92000145
              \&amp;rbrack;
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+van+Beek"}> Tim van Beek
              \: The two statemants above are a condensate of diverse papers I read, any input about the true state of the art would be most welcome. My educated guess is that the “mildness” of the assumptions indicates the fact that all physically realistic models are supposed to satisfy these.
          %p Should it be bounded or unbounded operators/observables?
          %ul
            %li
              %p Pro: Some observables do not have an upper bound, e.g. you can always increase the impulse of an electron, no matter how big that already is (in theory).
            %li
              %p Contra: A detector has an upper bound for every observable it can detect, and that is what is actually measured in an experiment. The situation of the pro-bullet won’t arise because we measure observables in bounded regions of spacetime, and the energy contained in a bounded region is always finite (we are excluding artifacts like black holes from our consideration, of course).
            %li
              %p Contra to even asking the question: Theories of bounded and unbounded operators/observables are physically equivalent modulo mathematical subtleties, meaning they produce the same numbers that can be compared to experiments.
          %p
            One simple situation where the construction of a Haag-Kastler net out of Wightman fields is straight forward is this: Suppose that all smeared field operators of the Wightman theory are essentially self adjoint for real test functions and commute strongly (their spectral projections commute) if the test functions have space-like separated support. Then we can define local algebras by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℳ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒪
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{M}(\mathcal{O}) := \{F(\Psi(f)) | f
            is a real test function with support contained in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒪
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{O}
            and F is a bounded Borel measurable function on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \R \}
          %p
            (Our assumptions allow us to use the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Borel_functional_calculus"} Borel functional calculus
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#neutral_real_uncharged_scalar_field Neutral Real (uncharged) Scalar Field
          %p
            A neutral real quantum scalar field on Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with mass parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \gt 0
            can be defined as follows:
          .un_def
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              The
              %strong positive mass shell
              is the subset of Minkowski spacetime defined by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi X
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 0
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X^+_m := \{p | p^2 = m^2, \; p_0 \gt 0 \}
            %p
              The
              %strong normalized Lorentz-invariant measure
              on the positive mass shell is defined with respect to the Lesbegue measure by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi d
                    %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi d
                          %mn 3
                        %mover
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mi π
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mn 3
                        %msub
                          %mi ω
                          %mi p
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext with
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi ω
                      %mi p
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %msqrt
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mover
                          %mi p
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %msup
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                          %mn 2
                        %mo +
                        %msup
                          %mi m
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    d\lambda(p) = d\lambda(\omega_p, \vec p) = \frac{d^3 \vec p}{(2\pi)^3 \omega_p} \; \text{with} \; \omega_p = p^0 = \sqrt{|\vec p|^2 + m^2}
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi X
                    %mi m
                    %mo +
                  %mo ,
                  %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = L^2(X^+_m, \lambda)
            be the Hilbert space with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi X
                    %mi m
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+_m
            the positive mass shell and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            the normalized Lorentz-invariant measure on it as defined above. Construct the Boson
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fock+space"} Fock space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s(H)
          %p
            Define the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            to be the Lorentz invariant Fourier transform restricted to
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi X
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^+_m
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R: \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R}^4) \to H
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi X
                        %mi m
                        %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext with
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi M
                  %msup
                    %mi e
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi μ
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi μ
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi dx
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R(f) := \hat f |_{X^+_m} \; \text{with} \; \hat f := \mathcal{F}(f) = \int_M e^{i p_{\mu} x^{\mu}} f(x) dx
          %p
            The quantum field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            is now a real tempered distribution on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            with values in the space of operators of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s(H)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo :
                  %mi 𝒮
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Psi:   \mathcal{S}(M) \to \mathcal{B}(F_s(H))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msqrt
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Rf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Rf
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  f \mapsto \Psi(f) = \frac{1}{\sqrt(2)} (a(Rf) + a^*(Rf))
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^*
            are the annihilation and creation operators on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_s(H)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(v)
            anihilates a single particle state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi a
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a^*(v)
            creates a single particle state
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            is a distribution solution of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
            by construction, that is for every test function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            we get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo □
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Psi((\Box + m^2) f) = 0
          %p The reason for this is that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo □
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msup
                    %mi m
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mtext on
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msubsup
                    %mi X
                    %mi m
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{F}{[(\Box + m^2) f]} = (-p^2 + m^2) f = 0 \; \text{on} \; X^+_m
          %h3#further_examples Further examples
          %p The Wightman axioms have been established for the following theories.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Massive 2d scalar theories with polynomial interactions, see
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/1970959"} this article
                by Glimm, Jaffe and Spencer.
            %li
              %p
                Massive
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4
                in 3d, see
                %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0003491676902232"} this article
                by Feldman and Osterwalder as well as this one by Magnen and Seneor.
            %li
              %p
                Massive Gross-Neveu in 2d see
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/DPubS?service=UI&version=1.0&verb=Display&handle=euclid.cmp/1104114300"} this article
                by Gawedzki and Kupiainen and
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/DPubS?service=UI&version=1.0&verb=Display&handle=euclid.cmp/1104114625"} this one
                by Feldman, Magnen, Rivasseau and Seneor.
            %li
              %p
                Massive Thirring model, see
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.5169/seals-114796"} this article
                by Frohlich and Seiler and this more recent one by Benfatto, Falco and Mastropietro.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"}> AQFT
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"}> conformal net
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+bootstrap"} conformal bootstrap
            %li
              %p
                analog for
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+field+theory"} Euclidean field theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Osterwalder-Schrader+axioms"} Osterwalder-Schrader axioms
          %h2#references References
          %p The original texts are
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A. S. Wightman, L. Gårding,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fields as Operator-valued Distributions in Relativistic Quantum Theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raymond+F.+Streater"}> Raymond F. Streater
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+S.+Wightman"} Arthur S. Wightman
                = succeed "," do
                  %em PCT, Spin and Statistics, and All That
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691070629/pct-spin-and-statistics-and-all-that"} ISBN:9780691070629
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctt1cx3vcq"} jstor:j.ctt1cx3vcq
          %p
            Raymond Streater relates some historical background about the book and the approach
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.mth.kcl.ac.uk/~streater/wightman.html"} on his webpage
          %p
            Introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QFT"} QFT
            via Wightman axioms and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Garth+Warner"}> Garth Warner
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Quantum Field Theory Seminar (School of Wightman et al.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://sites.math.washington.edu//~warner/QFTSeminar_Warner.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Warner-WightmanQFT.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franco+Strocchi"}> Franco Strocchi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Relativistic Quantum Mechanics and Field Theory
                %strong 34
                (2004) 501-527 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0401143"}> arXiv:hep-th/0401143
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            and with emphasis on its role in
            = succeed ":" do
